After double fertilisation in an angiosperm ovule, the cells carry three different ploidy levels:
- the **antipodals** (and synergids) come from the haploid megaspore, so they are **n**;
- the **zygote** forms when one male gamete fuses with the egg, so it is **2n**;
- the primary endosperm nucleus forms when the other male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei (triple fusion), so the **endosperm** is **3n**.

The nucellus and integuments are maternal diploid tissue, so the other options do not give n, 2n and 3n in order. So the answer is option (A) antipodal, zygote and endosperm.

In the transverse section of a young anther:
- A points to the central sterile tissue joining the anther lobes, the **connective** (with its vascular strand);
- B points to a layer of the anther wall just inside the epidermis, the **endothecium**;
- C points to the cells inside the microsporangium, the **pollen grains** (microspores).

So the answer is option (A) A – Connective, B – Endothecium, C – Pollen grain.

(a) Biodiversity hotspots are regions with a **very high level of species richness** and a **high degree of endemism** (species found nowhere else). Although these hotspots together cover less than 2 per cent of the earth's land area, the number of species they harbour is extremely high, so strict protection of these hotspots could reduce the ongoing mass extinctions by almost 30 per cent.

(b) Any two of the three hotspots that cover India's exceptionally high biodiversity regions:
- Western Ghats and Sri Lanka;
- Indo-Burma;
- Himalaya.

The sectional view of a mature pollen grain (male gametophyte) is drawn as a rounded grain with a two-layered wall and two cells inside. Any four labels:
- **exine**, the hard outer layer;
- **intine**, the thin, continuous inner layer;
- **germ pore**, a gap in the exine;
- **vegetative cell**, the large cell with an irregularly shaped nucleus;
- **generative cell**, the small spindle-shaped cell floating in the cytoplasm of the vegetative cell.

Functions (any two, each matched to its label):
- **Vegetative cell**: contains abundant food reserve for pollen germination and growth of the pollen tube.
- **Generative cell**: divides mitotically to produce the two male gametes.
- **Exine**: made of sporopollenin, one of the most resistant organic materials known, so it protects the pollen grain.
- **Intine**: made of cellulose and pectin; it grows out as the pollen tube.
- **Germ pore**: the region where sporopollenin is absent; the pollen tube emerges through it.

A schematic antibody molecule is drawn as a Y shape made of four polypeptide chains: two long **heavy chains** and two short **light chains**, so the molecule is represented as \(\text{H}_2\text{L}_2\). Any four labels:
- **antigen binding site**, at the tip of each arm;
- **light chain**;
- **heavy chain**;
- **disulphide bridges** (S-S bonds), which hold the chains together;
- **N terminal**, at the tips of the arms (the variable region);
- **C terminal**, at the base of the stem.

Antibodies are chemically **proteins** (immunoglobulins). They are produced by the **B-lymphocytes** (B-cells), which release them into the blood in response to pathogens.
